# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: iderunboardstop
# board: discover
# Stopping a program running on a board, and the board afterwards.
#
# A stop has to be a stop: the program ends, and the board is its own
# again -- its shell answering the namespace, not still inside the
# interpreter with a run nobody is watching.  That last part is what a
# leaked hold would fail, and it is the reason this script exists.
wait 60
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Programming
wait 4
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 150
expect-title Project
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
expect-title Open
click 400 220
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
expect-no-title Open
click-window Project
wait 8
note the row the list stands on is the long one
key Down
wait 8
note where runs go is chosen once, in Connect; the project remembers it
click 175 8
wait 8
menu-pick tiku
wait 10
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Run blink.bas on tiku
note it is still going: Stop is offered only while a run is, and the
note loop is a minute of the board's time
wait 200
click-window Project
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
expect-menu Stop
menu-pick Stop
wait 120
note and the board is its own again: its namespace answers, which it
note could not do from inside the interpreter or under a hold nobody let go
expect-read /devices/tiku/sys/device/name tiku
quit
